                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Tucson AZ",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued August 29 at 4:49PM MST until August 31 at 5:00AM MST by NWS Tucson AZ",
                "description": "* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Tucson Metro Area, Southeast Pinal County, Tohono O'odham\nNation, Upper San Pedro River Valley Upper Santa Cruz River\nValley/Altar Valley, Baboquivari Mountains, Catalina and Rincon\nMountains, Dragoon and Mule and Huachuca and Santa Rita Mountains.\n\n* WHEN...Through late Sunday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\nLow-water crossings may be flooded. Flooding may occur in poor\ndrainage and urban areas.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- An increase in moisture will bring scattered thunderstorms\nSaturday and Sunday. Rainfall rates in excess of 1 inch per hour\nare expected with the stronger storms with isolated areas having\nthe potential of 1-3 inches. Additional rainfall from the\nprevious day and overnight activity will result in faster\nresponses in normally dry washes, creeks and roadway dips.\n\n- Http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "You should monitor later forecasts and be prepared to take action\nshould Flash Flood Warnings be issued.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
                "parameters": {
                    "AWIPSidentifier": [
                        "FFATWC"
                    ],
